{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"07","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Evidence Collection","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting evidence collection to a manifest and named evidence files, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the evidence manifest shows a defensible decision in CAT 100"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","cat100_supplement_evidence_collection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_annotation_signal"],"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Evidence Collection\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Evidence Collection, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-07-evidence-collection.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Evidence Collection: Evidence Collection, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA folder full of files named `Untitled.docx` and `final final v2.docx` forces a reviewer to open everything and guess what each file proves. That guessing is where credit gets lost: a reviewer who cannot find the evidence for a claim treats the claim as unsupported. This section teaches you to assemble evidence as a named set of files plus a manifest that maps each file to the exact claim it backs, so anyone can open one file and immediately see what it demonstrates.\n\nThe discipline here is file management as argument. Each claim you want credit for should have exactly one file behind it, and each file should support exactly one claim. A claim with no file is unsupported; a file with no claim is clutter. The manifest makes both gaps visible, which is what turns a pile of work into a reviewable case. Descriptive naming plus a README-style manifest is the standard findability practice in research data management (the F in the FAIR principles; Wilkinson et al., 2016), not a course quirk.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the evidence manifest shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student finishes Module 4 and turns in a folder: `Untitled.docx`, `Screenshot 2026-05-18.png`, `final final v2.docx`, `notes.txt`. Every file might contain good work, but the reviewer cannot tell what any of it proves without opening all four and reading them. The screenshot proves nothing on its own. Two of the documents have nearly identical names, so the reviewer does not even know which is current.\n\nThe same work, organized, becomes a case: `01_persona.txt`, `02_factcheck_table.xlsx`, `03_parent_email_before_after.docx`, `04_decision_log.xlsx`, each name stating its content, plus a manifest saying what claim each file backs. Now the reviewer opens the table named `02_factcheck` and confirms in seconds that three AI claims were verified. The work did not change; the way it was made findable did.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvidence collection has two artifacts working together: named files and a manifest that maps each file to a claim.\n\n| Move | Bad version | Good version |\n|------|-------------|--------------|\n| File naming | `Untitled.docx`, `v2 final.docx` | `01_persona.txt`, name states the content |\n| Claim mapping | reviewer infers what each file is for | manifest row: file to the exact claim it proves |\n| Coverage | some claims have no file | every claim has exactly one file behind it |\n| Pruning | screenshots and stray notes pad the folder | files that prove nothing are cut |\n\nThe key idea is a one-to-one mapping between claims and files. Coverage means every claim you want credit for has a file; pruning means every file earns its place by backing a claim. A numbered prefix (`01_`, `02_`) gives the set a reading order, so the reviewer can follow your case from start to finish rather than opening files at random.\n\nA manifest is just a small table, often kept as a `README.txt` or a sheet, with two columns: the file name and the claim it proves. It is the index to your evidence. Without it, the reviewer reconstructs your argument by guessing; with it, they verify it by reading.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your manifest lists the claim ’I revised my persona after testing it,’ but you saved only the final persona and overwrote the draft. What does the coverage check tell you to do?\" explanation=\"The claim maps to no file, so the coverage check fails. The honest options are to create the missing evidence (save persona_v1.txt and persona_v2.txt) or drop the claim. Asserting ’I revised it’ with nothing a reviewer can open is exactly the unsupported claim the manifest is built to expose.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Either produce the before/after files or drop the claim; do not assert what no file can back\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Add three more screenshots so the folder looks fuller\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Keep the claim anyway, since you remember doing the revision\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Rename the final file to persona_v2.txt and call it proof of revision\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nEvidence collection is concrete file management: you build a manifest that maps each saved file to the exact claim it proves. A freshman assembles a folder and a manifest table so a reviewer can open any file and see what it supports. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. Here is the messy first folder a student turns in:\n\n```text\nmodule4/\n  Untitled.docx\n  Screenshot 2026-05-18.png\n  final final v2.docx\n  notes.txt\n```\n\nNothing names what it proves, so a reviewer has to open every file and guess.\n\nStep 2. Rename the files so the name states the content, then build a manifest mapping file to claim:\n\n| Renamed file | Claim it proves |\n|--------------|-----------------|\n| `01_persona.txt` | I wrote a four-field teacher persona |\n| `02_factcheck_table.xlsx` | I verified 3 AI claims and rejected the fabricated 90% statistic |\n| `03_parent_email_before_after.docx` | I revised a vague email into a concrete, dated one |\n| `04_decision_log.xlsx` | I overrode the AI twice and accepted once, with reasons |\n\nStep 3. Check coverage. Each claim you want credit for now has exactly one named file behind it. A claim with no file is unsupported; a file with no claim is clutter and gets cut.\n\nStep 4. Log the one rejected item. You had a fifth file, `Screenshot 2026-05-18.png`, but it proved nothing on its own, so you cut it rather than pad the folder. The renamed files plus the manifest are the evidence: a reviewer can open any row and confirm the claim without asking you.\n\n**Inside `04_decision_log.xlsx`.** The decision log is not just a filename; its rows are the artifact. Three columns are enough:\n\n| AI suggestion | Decision | Reason / evidence |\n|---------------|----------|-------------------|\n| Add \"studies show 90% of students improve\" to the persona rationale | Rejected | Could not verify the source; ties to the fabricated statistic caught in `02_factcheck_table.xlsx` |\n| Shorten the parent email's subject to name the date and topic | Accepted | Matches the concrete-subject rule; before/after saved in `03_parent_email_before_after.docx` |\n| Rewrite the persona to sound \"more authoritative\" | Overridden | Kept the bounded-source wording but adopted one clearer sentence; authority comes from the named standards, not tone |\n\nEach row names the suggestion, the call (accepted, overridden, or rejected), and the evidence a reviewer could check. That is what makes the log a claim-backing file rather than a label in the manifest.\n\n> **Recent evidence:** The AI suggestions recorded in a file like `04_decision_log.xlsx` typically come from current general-purpose assistants such as ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini, or Claude. Model versions change rapidly (as of 2026, Copilot runs on GPT-5.1 and Gemini on Gemini 3 Pro), so the decision log, not the tool name, is what travels. Consistent with Chapters 1 and 8, disclose substantive AI use in your evidence set; assistive use such as grammar or proofreading may not require disclosure, but undisclosed substantive use is an integrity violation.\n\nOrder the moves that turn a messy folder into a reviewable evidence set:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"List the claims you want credit for, before looking at the files\", \"Rename each file with a numbered prefix so the name states its content (01_persona.txt)\", \"Map each claim to exactly one named file in the manifest\", \"Run the coverage check: every claim has a file, and either supply or drop any claim that has none\", \"Prune any file that backs no claim, logging what you cut and why\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nNow assemble the manifest one decision at a time, predicting each call before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Map files to claims, then prune\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You face two files: ’Untitled.docx’ and ’final final v2.docx.’ What is the first move toward a reviewable set?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Rename each so the name states its content and reading order (01_persona.txt, 03_parent_email_before_after.docx)\",\"Leave the names and just open each to remember what they are\",\"Delete both and start over\",\"Add a screenshot to make the folder look complete\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Rename first. A name that states the content and a numbered prefix give the reviewer a reading order and let them know what a file is before opening it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Your folder still has ’Screenshot 2026-05-18.png,’ which maps to no claim. What does the pruning check say?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Cut it, because a file that backs no claim is clutter that lowers the signal\",\"Keep it, because more files look like more evidence\",\"Rename it 05_evidence.png and leave it unmapped\",\"Map it to all four claims at once\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Cut it. A file with no claim is clutter; a screenshot is evidence only if it is named and mapped to a specific claim, such as ’05_published_page.png proves the page is live.’\"},{\"reveal\":\"After renaming, mapping, and pruning, each of your four claims has exactly one named file and every file appears in the manifest. A reviewer can open any row and confirm the claim without asking you, which is the test of a reviewable evidence set.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nMap every claim to one named file and cut every file that maps to no claim. An evidence set is reviewable only when the manifest lets someone confirm each claim by opening a single file.\n\nRun these three checks before you submit:\n\n1. **Coverage check:** Does every claim you want credit for have exactly one file behind it?\n2. **Naming check:** Does each file name state its content, so a reviewer knows what it is before opening it?\n3. **Pruning check:** Does every file in the folder back a claim, or are there screenshots and stray notes proving nothing?\n\nIf a file does not appear in your manifest, it is clutter. If a claim has no file, it is unsupported. Fix both before you call the set complete.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that more files mean stronger evidence. A folder of twelve files where four are duplicates and three are unlabeled screenshots is weaker than four named files that each prove a claim. Padding lowers the signal a reviewer can find.\n\nThe second misreading is that a screenshot is automatically evidence. A screenshot proves something only if it is named and mapped to a claim (\"`05_published_page.png`, proves the page is live at the URL\"). An unlabeled `Screenshot 2026-05-18.png` proves nothing because the reviewer cannot tell what they are looking at or why.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the manifest method when a claim turns out to have no file. You list the claims you want credit for and find one says \"I revised my persona after testing it,\" but no file shows the before and after. The coverage check fails for that claim. You have two honest options: create the missing evidence by saving both `persona_v1.txt` and `persona_v2.txt`, or drop the claim because you cannot support it.\n\nSuppose you saved only the final persona and overwrote the draft. Then the claim \"I revised it\" is unsupported, and the right move is to drop it rather than assert it. The walkthrough shows the manifest doing real work: it does not just organize evidence you have, it exposes claims you cannot back, so you stop yourself from asserting something a reviewer would not be able to confirm. That is the integrity check built into the coverage step.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **the file that proves it** on the right. A strong reader does not say \"the folder is organized\"; they say \"the claim about verifying AI advice is backed by `02_factcheck_table.xlsx`, which I could open and confirm.\" The named file is what makes the claim checkable.\n\nA strong reader of Evidence Collection can answer four questions:\n\n1. Does every claim in the manifest map to exactly one file?\n2. Does every file in the folder appear in the manifest, or is something padding it?\n3. Which claim, if any, has no file behind it and should be dropped or supported?\n4. Could a reviewer confirm each claim by opening a single named file, without asking you?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can read the manifest and confirm each claim by opening one named file, with no orphan files and no unsupported claims. Keep the manifest as a `README.txt` or sheet at the top of the folder so it is the first thing a reviewer sees.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"Each of my four claims maps to one named file, and the manifest lets a reviewer open any row and confirm it. I cut `Screenshot 2026-05-18.png` because it proved nothing on its own and would have padded the folder without adding a verifiable claim.\" That states the one-to-one mapping and defends one pruning decision.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the manifest from a course folder to a job portfolio on GitHub Pages. The same logic governs: each portfolio item should map to a claim a hiring reviewer cares about (\"this repo shows I can build a working web page\"), and items that back no claim get cut. But the audience shifts the standard for what counts as a claim. A reviewer for credit cares that you completed the module; a hiring reviewer cares that you can do the skill again. So the manifest entries reframe from \"I did the assignment\" to \"this artifact demonstrates a transferable skill.\" The mapping discipline transfers; the claims you choose to support change with who is reading.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how the file-to-claim manifest connects to the chapter’s wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_evidence_collection\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the evidence manifest packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-07-evidence-collection-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"evidence manifest\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the evidence manifest.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the evidence manifest for CAT 100 04.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_evidence_collection\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that every claim maps to one named file, every file appears in the manifest, no claim is left unsupported, and you logged one file you cut and why.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Wilkinson, M. D., et al. (2016). The FAIR guiding principles for scientific data management and stewardship. Scientific Data. https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2016.18\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_collection","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_evidence_collection","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"Which filename lets a reviewer know what the file proves without opening it?","options":["final final v2.docx","02_factcheck_table.xlsx","Untitled.docx","Screenshot 2026-05-18.png"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A descriptive, ordered name states the content; 'Untitled' and 'final final v2' tell the reviewer nothing.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_meaningless_filenames","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_collection","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_evidence_collection"],"stem":"Your folder has four well-named files but no document linking them to claims. What is missing?","options":["More files.","A manifest mapping each file to the specific claim it proves.","A nicer folder color.","A longer reflection paragraph."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Without a file-to-claim manifest, the reviewer cannot tell what each file is evidence for.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_no_claim_mapping","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_evidence_collection"],"stem":"Your AI tutor suggests strengthening your evidence package with the line 'studies show 90% of students improve.' You search and cannot find a credible source. What belongs in your decision log?","options":["Add the line anyway; the tutor is usually right.","Record it as REJECTED, with the reason that the statistic could not be verified, and keep the row in the log.","Soften it to 'many students improve' so no source is needed.","Delete the suggestion from the log so the package stays clean."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The decision log is where AI suggestions meet evidence. A statistic with no verifiable source cannot enter the package in any wording; softening it just hides the same unverified claim. The defensible move is a logged rejection with its reason, which is itself evidence of your verification work (it ties to the 02_factcheck row).","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_unverified_ai_claim_kept","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-01","cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_evidence_collection_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat100_supplement_evidence_collection"],"stem":"In a complete manifest, what relationship should hold between claims and files?","options":["Many claims share one file.","Each claim you want credit for has at least one named file behind it.","There should be far more files than claims.","Claims and files do not need to match."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Every claim needs a supporting file; an unsupported claim and an unclaimed file are both defects.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_no_claim_mapping","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-02","cat100-supplement-04-07-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_meaningless_filenames","pattern":"uninformative_file_names","description":"Saving files as 'Untitled.docx' or 'final final v2' so the name does not say what the file contains or proves","trigger":"file names do not describe content or the claim they support","feedback":"Rename each file so the name states the content (for example 02_factcheck_table.xlsx). A reviewer should not have to open it to know what it is.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_no_claim_mapping","pattern":"files_without_claims","description":"Collecting a pile of files with no manifest linking each file to the specific claim it proves","trigger":"folder has files but no map from file to claim","feedback":"Build a manifest: one row per file naming the claim it supports. A claim with no file is unsupported; a file with no claim is clutter.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_more_is_better","pattern":"quantity_over_relevance","description":"Padding the folder with extra files (a stray screenshot) that prove nothing, assuming more files mean stronger evidence","trigger":"folder includes items that map to no claim","feedback":"Cut files that prove nothing on their own. Evidence strength comes from each file backing a claim, not from the file count.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_rename_as_proof","pattern":"overwrite_passed_off_as_revision","description":"Renaming or keeping only the final file and claiming it proves revision happened, when no before/after evidence exists.","trigger":"a claim of revision is backed by a single saved version","feedback":"Save both versions (persona_v1.txt and persona_v2.txt) or drop the claim. A single final file, whatever it is named, cannot back 'I revised it'; the evidence of revision is the pair a reviewer can compare.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_7_unverified_ai_claim_kept","pattern":"unverifiable_ai_claim_in_evidence","description":"Keeping an AI-suggested claim in the evidence package when its source cannot be verified.","trigger":"an AI-drafted statistic or citation enters the manifest without a verification row","feedback":"Every AI suggestion that touches the evidence package gets a decision-log row: accepted with its verified source, or rejected with the reason it could not be verified. An unverifiable statistic is rejected, not softened.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d32e9eac5dbf6ee2b5b3f52a56d6f64b1aec91d19e9d42a5803231610990ef5b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}